#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "11f5bedf-cfc8-5ace-b080-5ec30b406a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7436" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "29cfe1ff-ca64-51f9-8da1-5d0f798711f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7437" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "07678ad9-a16f-5fd2-8e39-85376aa9112e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7442" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3afa95b7-02e2-5ada-bc15-1419124e03ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285792} {:answer/id #uuid "5e742002-4a38-5ead-a25e-c6f22714768d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285793} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f5d15d-e732-5574-932f-1825df384cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285794} {:answer/id #uuid "f8434fc9-b25f-587e-a7fe-02ef17092fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285795} {:answer/id #uuid "711ac830-b9b2-50d0-b382-c8924c3f09bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3afa95b7-02e2-5ada-bc15-1419124e03ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285792} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8795698-e693-5154-b430-5eaeb64341d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "827bc944-9468-5f1f-aaa1-d206a7a58e66" :question/math-academy-id "q-106412" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97bfb9ef-3b21-59a5-b2de-dbce50578c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285799} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf26b1b-c1e2-5d0b-9c3a-0d41490094fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} {:answer/id #uuid "a370caac-e18e-5e57-8775-60e2285abcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285801} {:answer/id #uuid "474b91e9-76ab-55bc-bf3e-6da4e94fe85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285802} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4f9285-417b-56b7-9d67-fdcf50a6ede0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3t,\\,\\,y=6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a370caac-e18e-5e57-8775-60e2285abcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6db4438-92a4-525c-881f-a8f8e59c4c86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "200bd8e0-2a01-528c-8a4d-6d1ad4880558" :question/math-academy-id "q-106420" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=12x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =12x \\\\ 4p & =12 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd4ca16-aa3c-5748-aad7-a230bcfe1ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285806} {:answer/id #uuid "92aca7dd-cb69-5eee-bf37-14120c5c0341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285807} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd7f90d-dd26-546a-9eca-4107d0fae203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285808} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc6fdcd-5916-5657-898a-b0be08b1d218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285809} {:answer/id #uuid "2a065bab-a3f3-5969-a499-32bec8351be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd7f90d-dd26-546a-9eca-4107d0fae203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285808} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3214d6a-54c5-5849-a0af-4a15e8a0fc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5643ecf-db9f-54b9-a121-b654635dafb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106438" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2x,\\,{t}^{2}=-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =-4y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d42c5d5-80e3-580d-960e-7df227493003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d42c5d5-80e3-580d-960e-7df227493003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285813} :answer-field/id #uuid "85147514-3354-58a7-a1e6-279ac135e7ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be5cba92-c81d-5562-a82c-b4204abed9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be5cba92-c81d-5562-a82c-b4204abed9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285815} :answer-field/id #uuid "50614bfe-72ab-557f-b670-366e2ddcee47" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "716f582f-6ae3-52fc-944d-f0627997e4ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-274746" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/cdd531ad1df161f1c345c053c51e70fb522178f83688d1bdd07096bbdd40df00.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(2)t,\\,y=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efb0542a-ba57-513f-9121-1917818c05df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb0542a-ba57-513f-9121-1917818c05df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285818} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e01c186-4e0e-548b-9920-7319bffec2ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f800c495-45f0-573d-8c95-cc345545ac2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-274758" :question/problem "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=5{t}^{2},\\,y=10t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{5},\\,t=\\frac{y}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{100} & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =20x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=20x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa0af773-f3e9-534a-b32c-576890af5188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0af773-f3e9-534a-b32c-576890af5188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285821} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c658391-5b04-5f79-baf2-86fc7b670d04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79c45174-afa5-5c27-97c0-32cf7cbf276c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c45174-afa5-5c27-97c0-32cf7cbf276c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285823} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a28d4f-752f-54c7-a9dc-04dc60536d51" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7efad7ad-bfef-569f-a122-112bd419aa8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-280223" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-8x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-8x \\\\ 4p & =-8 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-2{t}^{2},\\,y=-4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9226366a-7e6c-57b9-bb09-42e28574e9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285826} {:answer/id #uuid "83ae55a8-424b-5db0-88c9-b4871442c9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9226366a-7e6c-57b9-bb09-42e28574e9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285826} :answer-field/id #uuid "52f049a9-3ae6-5a61-8dfa-a7bc4cf44ec7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52a3fd5e-b441-59a1-a747-68f07a6e511c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52a3fd5e-b441-59a1-a747-68f07a6e511c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285829} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1cceaff-b325-5200-b77e-b0afe0b09d34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7ad6dff-87f7-5dba-b694-74388096d2f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-280227" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=28x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =28x \\\\ 4p & =28 \\\\ p & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(7){t}^{2},\\,y=2(7)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2},\\,y=14t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bed0c2b0-24f9-5ccd-aba1-0e03715b8e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285832} {:answer/id #uuid "75c2fe08-1bcf-5695-a7cc-2aca55b78d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed0c2b0-24f9-5ccd-aba1-0e03715b8e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285832} :answer-field/id #uuid "9292f618-20fc-5f5b-b651-d46d9c09e736" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8960adf9-f3a6-585b-b81a-c530c948f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8960adf9-f3a6-585b-b81a-c530c948f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285835} :answer-field/id #uuid "146d0acb-1c7a-5699-899c-0a0fafd6cc94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "864c98fc-1abc-5908-a417-e5cb64cfdbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-280233" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{4}{5}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{4}{5})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{8}{5}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f04d4d4-2133-5dbc-a305-005aa794b32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285838} {:answer/id #uuid "a895497a-21cb-57fe-9900-9c04d3252d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a895497a-21cb-57fe-9900-9c04d3252d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285839} :answer-field/id #uuid "27dffcb9-9fe1-517a-9f95-abcde23c2b86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c403167e-2523-501b-884e-60c5c57d64a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-280365" :question/problem "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-10{t}^{2},\\,y=-5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x}{10},\\,t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x}{10}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{x}{10} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}x$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66c4d7b-b7e0-5427-aa09-67081702b893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087169} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf1d1aa-8710-52d1-b04f-7f24051c8c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{1}{8}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087170} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b1dd9f-3c72-53f9-b46f-31d8b8e53972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087171} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4b5790-ec0d-598d-8c52-1bf68082f1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{1}{8}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087172} {:answer/id #uuid "5c99c07f-958d-5da7-897e-51e78448baa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66c4d7b-b7e0-5427-aa09-67081702b893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087169} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b8130c0-7afd-5ddc-8dcc-1799688a8dc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ceae8e0-efff-58d4-996b-0a6fa5e6da13" :question/math-academy-id "q-84107" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-4x,\\,{t}^{2}=-8y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =16{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-8y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2} & =-8y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9171e1f2-a4c2-5f46-b8dc-a3634778f143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{3}{2}t, y = \\frac{3}{4}t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087175} {:answer/id #uuid "9865d43b-a784-5e4c-b3fb-c3ebeda0187a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-3t, y =-\\frac{3}{2}t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087176} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc11328-09c1-5388-a9ec-0ebaa2ee50f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{3}{2}t, y = \\frac{3}{2}t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087177} {:answer/id #uuid "eb908a75-710f-598f-87a1-dd8d0dc5888c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{3}{2}t, y =-\\frac{3}{4}t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087178} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff9fcc6-99e6-5adc-9bab-7bd9d17ce894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3t, y = 3t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb908a75-710f-598f-87a1-dd8d0dc5888c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{3}{2}t, y =-\\frac{3}{4}t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087178} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ace0707-472b-597f-8d17-f65a875b32a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc19725-defa-5a63-87f9-f8a8fdd626b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-84108" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87079e93-ff74-5a7d-93d6-1005ffe6003e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-6t^{2}, y =-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087187} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4046cd-31c2-585d-86cc-8f54c19baf40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 6t^{2}, y = 3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087188} {:answer/id #uuid "15a7318a-9584-5610-9fa2-a0ad6e0b47c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{6}t^{2}, y = \\frac{1}{3}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087189} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2676cd-2ff3-51d6-9a8b-f05cfda1b30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3t^{2}, y = 6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087190} {:answer/id #uuid "755ed6c1-b5c0-5d5b-a038-9e9e2c48ce65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{3}t^{2}, y =-\\frac{1}{3}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d2676cd-2ff3-51d6-9a8b-f05cfda1b30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3t^{2}, y = 6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087190} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ece82a-d85a-5681-b249-95519ba52434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3e4ab10-0c58-5645-afca-7eb9224cdeaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-84110" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/8e85666a72dde1ebbe24ff3d8dbb1404f010e81d9406aec790be11416b705f00.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(3,-6),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -6=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35493562-7d30-56dc-b6ba-6ff2a9cd800a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285842} {:answer/id #uuid "0728e5d9-c43b-58df-89f1-97fee14501b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285843} {:answer/id #uuid "c62885f3-8957-5e6f-815a-c14fce675bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285844} {:answer/id #uuid "34753177-4dac-5774-90ef-650d9e62bb43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285845} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0caedb-8a37-5dac-a5d9-5ba1678cbac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e0caedb-8a37-5dac-a5d9-5ba1678cbac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285846} :answer-field/id #uuid "3765f187-8258-5f3b-a4e2-95e68e6f1d94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34a25ab3-e3bb-53ed-b1e5-0084906affac" :question/math-academy-id "q-84674" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/671c38715438bb2a4b2ccf72a71765dadff1f2ed741ac52db7d4b70de43eee18.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(2,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,-4),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e88d0df-06b6-5434-8ee7-e41bd662943d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-7t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285849} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd72ae6-89a1-57ef-890b-9a8b14f83058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-7{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285850} {:answer/id #uuid "19eb8185-2670-53bd-a4a1-8cf6bfc406b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285851} {:answer/id #uuid "aee65ba4-52fa-5592-9962-0538b650591b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7t,\\,\\,y=-14{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285852} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce7f774-d75a-59d6-b1aa-0d0940d8bbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19eb8185-2670-53bd-a4a1-8cf6bfc406b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285851} :answer-field/id #uuid "4728aa8d-ba4c-59c6-bb37-aca322427c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51659ed2-0d6d-5995-abed-0cbe41395da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-84675" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-7,-7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-7,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-7$ and $y=-7$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7=2pt \\\\ -7=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{7}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =p{(-\\frac{7}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -7 & =p\\cdot \\frac{49}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -7 & =\\frac{49}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-7 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{7}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{7}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09323e57-5397-5ea7-9a92-7f28c9ccf23e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{8}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285856} {:answer/id #uuid "60302662-bc03-5cd2-b6ac-0c01c93e13e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285857} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f90974-6eee-5409-af59-f522677f0c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285858} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba985a2-d5c6-5416-a27e-c0a5c2febaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285859} {:answer/id #uuid "593fef5a-73aa-54b3-8d2b-c6b0ac6a7f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60302662-bc03-5cd2-b6ac-0c01c93e13e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285857} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b2437f-2ef6-5752-9517-36c471f988fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57d1b803-d96d-5b07-af56-e1bfc1202d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-84737" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=8y.$"}]}
